{%- comment -%}
  Outputs ONE word: what a listing's photographs actually show.

      {%- capture photos -%}{%- render 'photo-source', product: product -%}{%- endcapture -%}

  exact  the part itself was photographed
  donor  the pictures are of the donor vehicle the part came off, not of the part
  none   the listing has no photograph at all

  Some parts are listed with photographs of the donor vehicle rather than of the part.
  CoreYard names every real part photo <R#>_NN.jpg, where R# is the SKU; a donor photo is
  named <donor-key>_NN.jpg instead, so its filename stem is not the SKU. That catches
  legacy listings predating CoreYard's donor-photo note as well as new ones (checked
  against every listing carrying that note — the stem never matched the SKU — and against
  parts photographed individually, where it always did).

  This lived inline in main-product.liquid, where only the product page could reach it, so
  a card, a search result and the Product structured data all went on claiming the photo
  was the part. Anything that shows a listing's photograph asks here first.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign source = 'none'
  if product.featured_media
    assign source = 'exact'
    comment
      One variant per part, so the first variant's SKU is the part's R#. Fall back through
      selected_or_first_available_variant because a card renders no variant of its own.
    endcomment
    assign sku = product.selected_or_first_available_variant.sku | default: product.variants.first.sku
    if sku != blank
      assign photo_url = product.featured_media | image_url: width: 1
      assign photo_name = photo_url | split: '?' | first | split: '/' | last
      assign photo_stem = photo_name | split: '_' | first
      if photo_stem != blank and photo_stem != sku
        assign source = 'donor'
      endif
    endif
  endif
  echo source
-%}
